# Use the image CMD instead of replacing it with an idle keepalive.
#
# The harness runs the image CMD so services actually boot. The CMD is
# C:\entrypoint.ps1 and it is PID-1-safe:
#   * the drive-root DACL is normalized to desktop-Windows parity first - this
#     MUST happen at boot, because the Windows container runtime re-establishes
#     the volume root's security descriptor at mount time and discards any
#     build-time change to C:\ (see entrypoint.ps1 for the measurement). It is
#     NOT the seeded defect; it removes a base-image weakness that would
#     otherwise make every unquoted service path on the image exploitable;
#   * sshd + WinRM are started next;
#   * the vulnerability is a registry ImagePath (unquoted service path) on a
#     planted service - a config weakness, not a foreground process;
#   * PID 1 is an idle `while ($true) { Start-Sleep 60 }` loop.
#
# This scenario therefore REQUIRES the CMD to run. With the CMD replaced by an
# idle keepalive the root DACL is never normalized and the scenario is
# unsolvable, because verify.ps1 correctly scores C:\ as an offender.
#
# Consequence for solutions: the vulnerable service registration is ALREADY
# present, so a fix must quote the ImagePath (and/or remove the hijack binary);
# no process runs as PID 1 that a restart could kill.
